 Phishing and Social Engineering Awareness

 Introduction
Social engineering is a technique in which attackers manipulate people into revealing confidential information or taking unsafe actions. Phishing is a common example that uses deceptive messages or websites.

 Common Warning Signs
- Unexpected messages asking for passwords or personal details.
- Links leading to unfamiliar or misspelled website addresses.
- Messages creating fear or urgency.
- Unexpected attachments.
- Requests for money or verification codes.

 How to Stay Safe
1. Check the sender's address carefully.
2. Verify unusual requests using a separate trusted contact method.
3. Avoid opening suspicious links and attachments.
4. Never share passwords or one-time verification codes.
5. Report suspicious messages to your IT support team.

 Example
A message claims that your account will be closed unless you click a link immediately. Do not click the link. Visit the official website independently or contact the organization using a trusted number.

 Remember
Pretty words can hide dangerous intentions. Stop, think, and verify before acting.
